    "id": "news-1790768148882-0",
    "title": "Somali Woman's 17-Year Struggle with Obstetric Fistula Highlights Rural Health Crisis",
    "original_title": "15 Days Unconscious, 17 Years with Fistula: Shame, Fear, Isolation and a Journey Back to Hope.",
    "slug": "somali-woman-s-17-year-struggle-with-obstetric-fistula-highlights-rural-health-crisis",
    "summary": "A Somali woman known as Deeqa developed obstetric fistula at 14 after an obstructed labor, leading to 17 years of isolation and shame before she found treatment and hope.",
    "content": "At the age of 14, Deeqa experienced a harrowing childbirth ordeal in a rural area of Somalia. After eight days in labor without a skilled birth attendant, she was transported to Baidoa in an old pickup truck. She remained unconscious for 15 days, and during that time, her first two children, born when she was 14 and 15, did not survive. When she awoke, she discovered she had developed obstetric fistula, a condition that causes continuous urinary incontinence.\n\nObstetric fistula is a childbirth injury that typically results from prolonged or obstructed labor, often occurring when a woman has no access to emergency obstetric care. In Somalia, decades of conflict have weakened the healthcare system, particularly in rural regions where skilled birth attendants are scarce. Many women and girls face delays in reaching medical facilities, increasing the risk of fistula and other complications.\n\nAfter developing fistula, Deeqa faced 17 years of shame, fear, and isolation. The constant leaking of urine led to social stigma and physical discomfort, isolating her from her community and family. Like many Somali women with fistula, she likely suffered in silence due to the taboo surrounding the condition and a lack of awareness about available treatment.\n\nOver time, Deeqa's journey took a turn toward hope. She eventually accessed medical care that addressed her fistula, allowing her to reclaim her life. Her story underscores the importance of improving maternal healthcare access in Somalia, including training skilled birth attendants and ensuring emergency obstetric services are available in remote areas.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, Deeqa's experience resonates deeply. Many have relatives in Somalia who lack basic healthcare, and diaspora communities often support initiatives to build clinics and train healthcare workers. As diaspora members advocate for better maternal health, stories like Deeqa's highlight the need for continued investment in Somalia's health infrastructure and efforts to reduce stigma around fistula.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/09/30/15-days-unconscious-17-years-with-fistula-shame-fear-isolation-and-a-journey-back-to-hope/?utm_source=rss&#038;utm_medium=rss&#038;utm_campaign=15-days-unconscious-17-years-with-fistula-shame-fear-isolation-and-a-journey-back-to-hope",
    "source": "radiodalsan.com",
    "source_name": "Radio Dalsan",
    "original_url": "https://radiodalsan.com/2026/09/30/15-days-unconscious-17-years-with-fistula-shame-fear-isolation-and-a-journey-back-to-hope/?utm_source=rss&#038;utm_medium=rss&#038;utm_campaign=15-days-unconscious-17-years-with-fistula-shame-fear-isolation-and-a-journey-back-to-hope",
    "country": "Somalia",
    "category": "Health",
    "published_at": "2026-09-30T06:22:55.000Z",
    "first_seen_at": "2026-09-30T11:35:48.882Z",
