    "original_title": "Sideed badmaaxiin Masaari ah oo laga sii daayay afduub ka dhacay xeebaha Soomaaliya",
    "slug": "eight-egyptian-sailors-released-after-months-in-captivity-off-somalia",
    "summary": "Egypt's Foreign Ministry says eight Egyptian crew members abducted from the vessel M/T Eureka have been freed, months after armed men seized the ship off Puntland's coast.",
    "content": "Eight Egyptian sailors who had been held captive for several months after their vessel was hijacked near the coast of Somalia's Puntland region have been released, according to Egypt's Ministry of Foreign Affairs. The crew members were taken from the M/T Eureka on 2 May, at a time when the ship was reported to be sailing in waters beyond the Puntland coastline.\n\nAccounts indicate that armed men, believed to be Somali pirates, boarded the vessel and took control of it before redirecting it toward the Somali shore. The sailors remained in captivity for a period spanning multiple months before their release was confirmed by Egyptian authorities.\n\nThe M/T Eureka case is one of a number of incidents in recent years in which commercial vessels have been targeted in waters off the Horn of Africa. Somali piracy had declined sharply from its peak in 2011, when international naval patrols and improved onboard security measures curtailed most attacks, but a resurgence of hijackings has been recorded since 2023, often involving crews from Asian and Middle Eastern countries.\n\nPuntland authorities and international naval forces have periodically carried out operations aimed at intercepting hijacked ships and securing the release of crews. The circumstances surrounding the release of the Egyptian sailors — including whether a ransom was paid or whether any intermediaries were involved — have not been publicly detailed by Egypt's Foreign Ministry.\n\nFor Somali diaspora communities, news of hijackings and hostage cases carries particular weight. Many Somalis abroad maintain close ties to coastal regions and act as informal intermediaries between families, clan elders, and foreign governments seeking the release of captured crews. Prolonged hostage situations can also affect the reputation of Somali-run businesses and shipping services in the Gulf and Red Sea corridors, where diaspora firms are active.\n\nThe release of the eight crew members removes one immediate concern for their families, though the broader pattern of attacks in Somali waters remains unresolved. Monitoring groups continue to track incidents in the region, and ship operators have been advised to maintain heightened security measures while transiting the area.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/30/sideed-badmaaxiin-masaari-ah-oo-laga-sii-daayay-afduub-ka-dhacay-xeebaha-soomaaliya/",
