    "title": "Somalia's Electoral Commission Meets Hirshabelle Political Parties to Discuss Election Compliance",
    "original_title": "Guddiga Doorashooyinka iyo Ururrada Siyaasadda oo Jowhar kaga wada hadlay adkeynta habraacyada doorashada",
    "slug": "somalia-s-electoral-commission-meets-hirshabelle-political-parties-to-discuss-election-compliance",
    "summary": "The Independent Electoral and Boundaries Commission met with political parties in Jowhar to strengthen adherence to election laws and procedures ahead of upcoming parliamentary and local polls.",
    "content": "JOWHAR, September 29, 2026 — The Deputy Chairperson of Somalia's Independent Electoral and Boundaries Commission (IEBC), Saadaq Abshir Garaad, along with other commission members, held a meeting in Jowhar with leaders and representatives of political parties based in Hirshabelle State. The discussions centered on reinforcing compliance with electoral laws, procedures, and the code of conduct, as well as enhancing collaboration between the commission and political organizations.\n\nThe meeting comes as Somalia prepares for indirect elections for both the House of the People and local councils. In recent months, the IEBC has been engaging with regional authorities and political stakeholders to address disputes over election timelines, delegate selection, and the role of regional administrations. Hirshabelle, like other federal member states, plays a critical role in the indirect electoral process, where regional assemblies and clan delegates participate in selecting parliamentarians.\n\nKey figures at the talks included Saadaq Abshir Garaad, who has been at the forefront of the commission's outreach efforts. Political party leaders in Hirshabelle have previously expressed concerns about transparency and the need for consistent application of rules. The Jowhar meeting is part of a series of consultations aimed at building trust and ensuring that all parties understand their responsibilities under the national electoral framework.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, these developments are significant because many diaspora members maintain strong ties to their home regions and closely follow political processes that affect representation and governance. A credible and peaceful election in Hirshabelle and other regions can contribute to stability, which in turn influences diaspora investment, remittances, and political engagement. Conversely, any breakdown in the electoral process could exacerbate clan tensions and delay much-needed political reforms.\n\nThe IEBC's push for stricter adherence to electoral norms reflects broader efforts to improve the integrity of Somalia's indirect elections, which have often been criticized for corruption and manipulation. As the process moves forward, the commission's ability to enforce rules and foster cooperation among political parties will be closely watched by both domestic and international observers.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/29/guddiga-doorashooyinka-iyo-ururrada-siyaasadda-oo-jowhar-kaga-wada-hadlay-adkeynta-habraacyada-doorashada/",
